The node before the target is not available, so the usual previous-pointer method cannot be used. Instead, copy the next node's value into the current node, then skip over the next node.

The problem guarantees that the given node is not the tail. The input list is changed directly.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void deleteNode(ListNode* node) {
        if (node == nullptr || node->next == nullptr) {
            return;
        }

        ListNode* nextNode = node->next;
        node->val = nextNode->val;
        node->next = nextNode->next;
    }
};
```

For example, with `4 -> 5 -> 1`, if the node containing `5` is given, its value becomes `1` and its next pointer becomes `nullptr`. The list now appears as `4 -> 1`.

Time complexity: `O(1)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The missing previous pointer is the main detail here. Rather than trying to unlink the given node, copy the next node's value into it and connect it to the next node's successor.

This works only when a next node exists, which is why deleting the final node cannot be done with this method. The guard also covers a null reference and leaves the list unchanged in that case.

```csharp
class Solution {
    public void DeleteNode(ListNode node) {
        if (node == null || node.next == null) {
            return;
        }

        ListNode nextNode = node.next;
        node.val = nextNode.val;
        node.next = nextNode.next;
    }
}
```

A quick check with `1 -> 4 -> 6`, deleting the node at value `4`: the node first takes value `6`, then its `next` becomes null. The result is `1 -> 6`.

The input list is changed directly. No traversal from the head is needed.

Time complexity: `O(1)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The given node cannot be removed by changing its previous node because that previous node is not provided. The small trick is to make the given node look like its successor, then remove the successor from the chain.

The null checks are only defensive. A valid input node is guaranteed to have a next node, but the checks keep the method safe for an invalid call.

```java
class Solution {
    public void deleteNode(ListNode node) {
        if (node == null || node.next == null) {
            return;
        }

        node.val = node.next.val;
        node.next = node.next.next;
    }
}
```

This updates the existing list rather than creating a new one. It also means the original target node object stays in memory, but its value and link now represent the node that followed it.

Time: `O(1)` because only one next node is inspected.
Space: `O(1)` extra space.

